STOPS = [
    dict(id='5.1', title='The centre: command or corpse?',
         q='Why can a centred frame feel powerful or dead?',
         why='The centre is not neutral. Put a face, vanishing point or logo there and the viewer may feel ceremony, authority, entrapment or a joke that has stopped moving. The same geometry can become a shrine or a trap depending on what the subject and the world do inside it.',
         plates=[dict(key='p51_wave', cap='The small Fuji is not centred, but the wave creates an enclosing frame that makes it feel inevitable. Look for the route, not a grid.')],
         story=[
             'Symmetry gives the eye a stable promise: left and right will answer each other. One-point perspective adds a second promise, that receding lines will pull toward one vanishing point. Kubrick repeatedly uses that pull in corridors, roads and institutional spaces. The result is not automatically beautiful. It can feel ceremonial in a frontal icon, controlled in a Wes Anderson set, or menacing when a person appears too small inside a rigid system.',
             'The useful question is what the centre means in this scene. In an icon or altarpiece, frontality can make a figure present and authoritative. In <i>2001: A Space Odyssey</i> or <i>The Shining</i>, the same centred axis can make the viewer feel watched, swallowed or unable to escape. Anderson extends the device through set design, costume, colour and camera placement, so the frame feels designed all the way down. Move one actor, break one line or let one object refuse symmetry, and the disruption becomes a story event.',
             'For a web hero, centre a subject when the brand wants authority, ritual or a direct encounter. Offset it when the empty side needs to hold copy, a gaze, a route or a threat. Try three versions: centred, off-centre looking into space, and off-centre looking out. Keep the image that gives the empty area a job. A centred frame feels dead when nothing in it resists, answers or changes the axis. Symmetry also magnifies production mistakes: a crooked horizon, an uneven gap or an actor who has no relation to the vanishing point becomes visible immediately. That is useful pressure when the design can carry it, but a bad reason to imitate a famous director.',
         ],
         look=[('Stanley Kubrick, <i>The Shining</i>', 'Watch the vanishing point turn architecture into psychological pressure.', 'https://slate.com/culture/2012/08/stanley-kubrick-and-one-point-perspective-watch-a-thrilling-supercut-of-stanley-kubricks-favorite-symmetrical-composition.html'), ('Wes Anderson, <i>The Grand Budapest Hotel</i>', 'Notice symmetry spreading from camera to production design and movement.', 'https://revistas.ucm.es/index.php/ARIS/en/article/view/78848'), ('Katsushika Hokusai, <i>The Great Wave off Kanagawa</i>', 'The wave encloses Fuji, proving that a frame can be balanced without centring its subject.', 'https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/hokusai-great-wave')],
         steal=['Centre the enquiry form for a direct, trustworthy lodge or clinic decision, then break the axis when the visitor needs to explore.', 'Make one deliberate asymmetry in an otherwise ordered page: a species, headline or doorway that changes what the centre means.'],
         director='Use the centred vanishing point as a promise, then let blocking, sound or one break in the set reveal whether the world is safe, ritualised or hostile.',
         studio='For a safari landing page, a centred animal can feel iconic. Give the surrounding habitat or a directional gaze enough room if the real message is place, not trophy.',
         sources=[('Columbia film glossary, deep focus and long takes', 'https://filmglossary.ccnmtl.columbia.edu/term/long-take/'), ('Slate, Kubrick and one-point perspective', 'https://slate.com/culture/2012/08/stanley-kubrick-and-one-point-perspective-watch-a-thrilling-supercut-of-stanley-kubricks-favorite-symmetrical-composition.html'), ('Blanco-Perez, Wes Anderson and architecturisation', 'https://revistas.ucm.es/index.php/ARIS/en/article/view/78848'), ('Met, Hokusai', 'https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/hokusai-great-wave')]),

    dict(id='5.2', title='Diagonal voltage',
         q='What do diagonals do to your nerves?',
         why='A horizontal can rest and a vertical can stand. A diagonal looks as if it has left both jobs behind. That small refusal creates motion, imbalance and expectation, which is why painters, poster designers and cinematographers use it when a still image must feel like an event.',
         plates=[dict(key='p52_rubens', cap='Trace the rising bar of bodies, cloth and timber. The diagonal does not decorate the action, it is the action.')],
         story=[
             'In Rubens, bodies and drapery climb, collide and pull against the rectangle. In Géricault\'s <i>The Raft of the Medusa</i>, the wrecked figures form a rising diagonal toward the tiny ship on the horizon, so hope and desperation share one route. Caravaggio uses gestures and beams of light as vectors. Constructivist posters such as Rodchenko\'s <i>Books!</i> turn the diagonal into a public command: type, face and red shape all push outward.',
             'A Dutch angle, the tilted camera horizon, is the filmic cousin of the diagonal. It can suggest instability, intoxication, threat or a world whose rules have slipped. But a diagonal does not have one universal nervous effect. A mountain path may invite movement; a falling body may warn; a slash through a logo may merely make the logo harder to read. Direction comes from the relation between the line, the frame and the subject. Try rotating the same crop by five degrees, then by fifteen. At the smaller tilt you may feel energy; at the larger one the image may announce danger or parody. The threshold depends on what the viewer recognises as stable, and on whether the subject gives the tilt a reason.,',
             'Build one dominant diagonal before adding ten minor ones. Let a trail point toward an animal, an arm point toward a witness, or a beam point toward the fact the character wants hidden. Then remove any line that competes without adding a relationship. On a page, a diagonal can connect a headline to proof or create a fast editorial entry, but the reading order must still survive on a phone.',
         ],
         look=[('Peter Paul Rubens, <i>The Elevation of the Cross</i>', 'Follow the cross and the bodies as one muscular vector.', 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_Raising_of_the_Cross_-_1610.jpg'), ('Théodore Géricault, <i>The Raft of the Medusa</i>', 'Look from the dead foreground toward the living figures and the distant ship.', 'https://www.louvre.fr/en/explore/the-palace/the-raft-of-the-medusa'), ('Alexander Rodchenko, <i>Books!</i>', 'The diagonal makes typography behave like a voice calling from outside the poster.', 'https://www.moma.org/collection/works/78718'), ('Caravaggio, <i>The Calling of Saint Matthew</i>', 'A light beam and pointing hand join attention across a dark room.', 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calling-of-st-matthew.jpg')],
         steal=['Give a campaign one strong route from problem to proof, rather than decorating every edge with arrows.', 'Tilt only when the story needs instability, speed or a changed point of view.'],
         director='Block bodies and camera movement around a vector. A cut can complete an eyeline or turn the same diagonal from escape route into trap.',
         studio='Use a trail, branch or river to lead a visitor toward habitat and evidence, not automatically toward the booking button.',
         sources=[('Khan Academy, Caravaggio and the Baroque', 'https://www.khanacademy.org/humanities/renaissance-reformation/baroque-art1/italy/a/caravaggio-calling-of-st-matthew'), ('Louvre, The Raft of the Medusa', 'https://www.louvre.fr/en/explore/the-palace/the-raft-of-the-medusa'), ('MoMA, Rodchenko, Books!', 'https://www.moma.org/collection/works/78718'), ('Met, Rubens collection search', 'https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search#!?searchField=All&sortBy=Relevance&artist=Rubens')]),

    dict(id='5.3', title='Light chooses the witness',
         q='How does light stage attention?',
         why='Light is not a shower switched on so the audience can see the set. It selects faces, erases evidence, builds planes and changes the order in which a room can be read. Darkness can be concealment, intimacy, power or simply a badly exposed image.',
         plates=[dict(key='p53_caravaggio', cap='Find the beam, the pointing hand and the face that receives both. Caravaggio makes attention visible as a chain.')],
         story=[
             'Chiaroscuro models form through strong light and shade. Tenebrism pushes that concentration further, letting figures emerge from a mostly dark field. In Caravaggio\'s <i>The Calling of Saint Matthew</i>, a beam from the upper right joins Christ\'s gesture to Matthew. Rembrandt\'s <i>The Night Watch</i> is not just a black background with a spotlight: faces, hands, costume and movement receive different amounts of emphasis across a crowded group. Georges de La Tour uses a candle as both source and compositional clock, making still faces wait around a small flame.',
             'Film noir inherits the vocabulary but not a fixed mood dictionary. Low-key light may make a person dangerous, private, trapped or merely hard to read. Gordon Willis\'s work on <i>The Godfather</i> uses top light, pools of brightness and dark backgrounds so that Brando\'s eyes disappear in the opening office scene. The technique works because the viewer still has anchors: the hand, the desk, the voice, the bright background and the social power of the room.',
             'Make a value map before choosing a colour palette. Squint at the frame or blur it, then ask what wins first, second and last. For a site, preserve readable text contrast and let one dark field make one signal glow. For a scene, decide which eyes must be visible and which can remain withheld. Darkness is composition only when the viewer knows what kind of ignorance the darkness creates. A hidden face can make a powerful person unreadable, but it can also erase a performer\'s expression or a user\'s essential information. The ethical and practical test is the same: name what is withheld, what remains available and what the viewer is expected to infer.',
         ],
         look=[('Caravaggio, <i>The Calling of Saint Matthew</i>', 'A directional beam turns a gesture into narrative.', 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calling-of-st-matthew.jpg'), ('Rembrandt, <i>The Night Watch</i>', 'Map the hierarchy of faces, hands and movement across the group.', 'https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/SK-C-5'), ('Georges de La Tour, <i>The Penitent Magdalene</i>', 'See how a small candle creates a whole quiet world.', 'https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436839'), ('Gordon Willis, <i>The Godfather</i>', 'Compare readable darkness with uncontrolled underexposure.', 'https://theasc.com/post/the-film-book/gordon-willis-tribute-the-godfather/')],
         steal=['Make a two or three value thumbnail of every hero image before polishing colour.', 'Give darkness a job, such as hiding a face, isolating a fact or making a warm practical source feel precious.'],
         director='Borrow the logic, not the cliché. Motivate a source, control falloff and decide which information arrives now and which waits.',
         studio='A dark eco-lodge page can feel intimate, but the booking path, rates and accessibility text must remain legible. Dramatic contrast is never an excuse for low contrast body copy.',
         sources=[('National Gallery, Caravaggio', 'https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/artists/michelangelo-merisi-da-caravaggio'), ('Rijksmuseum, The Night Watch', 'https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/SK-C-5'), ('ASC, Gordon Willis tribute', 'https://theasc.com/post/the-film-book/gordon-willis-tribute-the-godfather/'), ('BFI, Gordon Willis', 'https://www.bfi.org.uk/features/gordon-willis-career-12-pictures')]),

    dict(id='5.4', title='The bull after the details leave',
         q='What can disappear before the bull stops being a bull?',
         why='Abstraction is not the removal of decoration until a logo looks fashionable. It is a search for the few cues that keep identity alive. Picasso lets us watch that search happen across eleven states, from mass and muscle to a handful of lines.',
         plates=[dict(key='p54_bull', cap='Use this older bull image as a category cue, then compare Picasso\'s successive reductions through the linked study pages.')],
         story=[
             'Picasso\'s <i>The Bull</i> lithograph sequence runs through eleven states made from December 1945 to January 1946. The early bull has weight, shading and observed anatomy. Later states remove descriptive information while preserving the horns, spine, muzzle and stance as cues. The lesson is procedural: reduction is a chain of tests, not a mystical flash of minimalism. Irving Lavin\'s Institute for Advanced Study essay is useful because it treats the sequence as thinking made visible.',
             'The same problem appears in logos and icons. A mark must remain recognisable across distance, size and context, but recognisability is not the only job. An icon may need to signal an action, a brand may need to be distinguishable from its competitors, and an illustration may need to keep a species or place ethically specific rather than turning it into a generic symbol. Remove noise, not the cue that carries memory.',
             'Make five versions: observed detail, grouped masses, contour, symbolic shape and minimum cue. Test each at thumbnail size and ask someone who did not see the source to name it. Stop reducing at the first break in recognition, then restore only what the audience needs. Minimal is a result of solving the communication problem, not a virtue that outranks meaning. A reduction can preserve category while losing individuality, or preserve a silhouette while losing an action. Test the mark in context: beside other icons, at low contrast, in motion and with no explanatory label. The right final state is the one that carries the intended idea under the real conditions of use.',
         ],
         look=[('Pablo Picasso, <i>The Bull</i>, state XI', 'Compare the final line with the earlier states, not with a generic idea of minimalism.', 'https://www.moma.org/collection/works/63062'), ('Irving Lavin, <i>Picasso and the Bull</i>', 'Read the sequence as a record of decisions about what recognition needs.', 'https://publications.ias.edu/sites/default/files/Lavin_PicassoBulls_2010.pdf'), ('IBM, Paul Rand logo, 1972', 'The broken letterforms remain legible through implied contour and repetition.', 'https://www.ibm.com/ibm/history/ibm100/us/en/icons/logo/'), ('Picasso, <i>Bull\'s Head</i>', 'A few found forms can trigger a complete category when the cue is distinctive.', 'https://www.moma.org/collection/works/68896')],
         steal=['Design the smallest useful version of every safari species icon before making the large illustration.', 'Test recognition without the brand name, then test memory after the name is removed.'],
         director='Reduction can turn a body into a sign. Preserve one characteristic gesture or silhouette and let the audience complete the rest.',
         studio='Use a recognisable animal silhouette as a recurring wayfinding cue, but pair it with words and habitat detail so biodiversity does not collapse into a mascot.',
         sources=[('Norton Simon, first state of The Bull', 'https://www.nortonsimon.org/art/detail/M.1977.08.3.01.G'), ('Norton Simon, eleventh state of The Bull', 'https://www.nortonsimon.org/art/detail/M.1977.08.3.11a,b.G'), ('MoMA, Picasso, The Bull state XI', 'https://www.moma.org/collection/works/63062'), ('Institute for Advanced Study, Lavin essay', 'https://publications.ias.edu/sites/default/files/Lavin_PicassoBulls_2010.pdf')]),

    dict(id='5.5', title='The camera is a composing machine',
         q='What does a camera add to composition?',
         why='A camera does not merely record a composition that already exists. Lens, distance, aperture, frame and movement decide which relationships can coexist, which edges disappear and how long the viewer gets to search.',
         plates=[dict(key='p55_meninas', cap='Look through the layers: foreground figures, the central group, the mirror and the doorway. Depth is a sequence of permissions to look.')],
         story=[
             'A wide lens can exaggerate distance and make near and far objects argue. A long lens can flatten relationships and isolate a chosen plane. Aperture changes depth of field, while camera distance changes perspective, so the phrase shallow focus is not a magic mood button. Shot size also changes the body\'s meaning: a close-up makes a face an event, a wide shot gives the environment evidence and a medium shot lets gesture and social space negotiate.',
             'Framing within the frame, through a doorway, window, branch or mirror, makes the edge do two jobs. It directs attention and implies a world beyond the visible rectangle. In <i>Citizen Kane</i>, deep focus lets foreground and background carry simultaneous information, while Spielberg often uses blocking and reframing to reveal information inside a long take. The 180-degree rule is a continuity convention that preserves screen direction across a cut. Crossing it can disorient, but that disorientation is a tool when the scene needs a new relation.',
             'Before shooting, write the job of the lens rather than its reputation: isolate a witness, keep habitat legible, compress a crowd or make a corridor swallow a body. Deakins treats natural light as designed and Lubezki often uses camera movement and available-feeling sources to keep space alive. Test the same action wide, normal and long, then ask what information each version adds or suppresses. Composition includes the camera\'s exclusions.',
         ],
         look=[('Orson Welles and Gregg Toland, <i>Citizen Kane</i>', 'Deep focus makes foreground, middle ground and background compete for meaning.', 'https://www.rogerebert.com/roger-ebert/a-viewers-companion-to-citizen-kane'), ('Diego Velazquez, <i>Las Meninas</i>', 'The painting behaves like a camera problem about viewpoint, mirror and off-screen space.', 'https://www.museodelprado.es/en/the-collection/art-work/las-meninas-or-the-family-of-philip-iv/9a2f6c36-3a85-4d1f-8c63-5b7f4b5f1dcb'), ('Roger Deakins, official site', 'Read how a natural-looking source is shaped for lens, actor and camera.', 'https://www.rogerdeakins.com/'), ('Emmanuel Lubezki, <i>The Revenant</i>', 'Watch movement, horizon and available-feeling light turn landscape into a participant.', 'https://www.ascmag.com/articles/the-revenant')],
         steal=['Choose a lens by the relationship it creates between person and place, not by a fashionable focal length.', 'Make one frame within the frame do narrative work, then leave the rest of the depth quiet.'],
         director='Write a visual arc for lens, focus and movement. A change of distance or focus can be the cut even when the shot does not cut.',
         studio='On a lodge site, pair a wide habitat image with a close detail and a human-scale activity image. Do not ask one crop to carry all three jobs.',
         sources=[('Columbia film glossary, long take and deep focus', 'https://filmglossary.ccnmtl.columbia.edu/term/long-take/'), ('Roger Ebert, viewer\'s companion to Citizen Kane', 'https://www.rogerebert.com/roger-ebert/a-viewers-companion-to-citizen-kane'), ('Roger Deakins official site', 'https://www.rogerdeakins.com/'), ('American Society of Cinematographers, Lubezki', 'https://www.ascmag.com/articles/the-revenant')]),

    dict(id='5.6', title='One composition, three distances',
         q='How does a layout survive a phone, a poster and a billboard?',
         why='A layout is not a miniature painting that gets scaled forever. At each size, the viewer has a different distance, time budget, crop and reading path. Responsive composition preserves the message by changing the arrangement, not by hoping the original artboard shrinks politely.',
         plates=[dict(key='p56_wave', cap='Crop the wave three ways. Preserve the tiny Fuji, the boat scale or the crest direction, and you get three different stories.')],
         story=[
             'At phone distance, the first job is orientation: what is this, where am I and what can I do? A poster may be read while walking, so silhouette, contrast and a few words must carry the message. A billboard is seen from farther away and for less time, so one large relationship beats a paragraph. A desktop page can support simultaneous image, copy, navigation and proof. The composition changes because the act of looking changes.',
             'Start with content priority, not breakpoints. Identify the one thing that must survive, the second thing that supports it and the details that can become a tap, fold or caption. Then change crop, type scale, column count, line length and image position at each context. A responsive system is not one frozen grid. It is a family resemblance across states, with explicit decisions about what disappears.',
             'Use the squint test as a studio heuristic: blur or stand back, and ask whether the intended focal mass still wins. It catches accidental logos, muddy contrast and a subject lost in a crop. It is not accessibility evidence and cannot replace real testing. Check phones in bright light, translated copy, zoom, keyboard use and slow loading. The billboard and the phone should share a promise, not necessarily a layout. A useful handoff includes the invariant, the controlled changes and the failure state: what must never be cropped, what can move below the fold and what happens when the headline becomes two lines in another language. That turns responsiveness from rescue work into composition planned over time.',
         ],
         look=[('Charles and Ray Eames, <i>Powers of Ten</i>', 'Scale changes the viewer\'s relationship to the same subject.', 'https://www.eamesoffice.com/the-work/powers-of-ten/'), ('Katsushika Hokusai, <i>The Great Wave off Kanagawa</i>', 'Compare a wide environmental read with a tight crop that turns the crest into an abstract sign.', 'https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/hokusai-great-wave'), ('W3C, Web Content Accessibility Guidelines', 'A composition that survives size must also survive zoom, contrast and different modes of access.', 'https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/'), ('Nielsen Norman Group, responsive design', 'Use responsive patterns as content decisions, not only technical breakpoints.', 'https://www.nngroup.com/articles/responsive-web-design-definition/')],
         steal=['Make phone, desktop and poster thumbnails before polishing any one version.', 'Write a survival sentence: if the viewer sees only one image shape and six words, what must remain?'],
         director='Think of aspect ratio and viewing distance as part of the shot. Protect the visual event first, then let secondary information move or vanish.',
         studio='For a safari client, keep species, place and action visible in the phone crop. Put itinerary detail below or behind a clear interaction rather than shrinking it into unreadable cards.',
         sources=[('Wolfe and Horowitz, visual attention review', 'https://doi.org/10.1038/nrn1411'), ('W3C, WCAG 2.2', 'https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/'), ('Nielsen Norman Group, responsive web design', 'https://www.nngroup.com/articles/responsive-web-design-definition/'), ('Met, Hokusai', 'https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/hokusai-great-wave')]),

    dict(id='5.7', title='When data becomes a picture',
         q='How does data become a legible picture?',
         why='A chart is a composition with consequences. It chooses what is compared, what is connected and what can be ignored. The best historical examples do not make numbers pretty first. They give the viewer a route through a claim.',
         plates=[dict(key='p57_minard', cap='Minard combines map, direction, troop width, dates and temperature. The narrowing band makes attrition visible as a spatial event.'), dict(key='p57_nightingale', cap='Nightingale\'s wedges turn monthly causes of death into a shape that argues for sanitation.')],
         story=[
             'Charles Joseph Minard\'s 1869 map of Napoleon\'s 1812 campaign combines geography, direction, troop size, dates and temperature. The band narrows as the army loses people, then the cold scale below connects weather to the retreat. It is not a decorative map with numbers added. Its visual variables are selected to make a historical narrative answerable: where did the force go, when did it shrink and under what conditions?',
             'Florence Nightingale\'s 1858 polar area chart, often called a rose diagram, compares preventable disease deaths with deaths from wounds and other causes by month. W. E. B. Du Bois and his collaborators made a suite of charts for the 1900 Paris Exposition, including maps, lines, bars and circular forms about Black life and progress. Their visual richness is part of the argument: data can show a population as a society with history, not as an anonymous statistic. Read the source and encoding before praising the style.',
             'Tufte\'s data-ink and graphical-integrity ideas are useful heuristics, not commandments. Remove elements that distract from the comparison, but keep labels, context, uncertainty and a legend when they let the reader check the claim. For a conservation dashboard, decide the question first, choose the visual variable second and decorate last. If the viewer cannot say what changed, by how much and compared with what, the picture has not become legible yet. Also separate reading from persuasion. A chart can be easy to read and still use a misleading baseline, selective period or overloaded colour key. Let the reader inspect the source, units and denominator. Legibility is the first promise; truthful comparison is the promise that makes the picture worth trusting.',
         ],
         look=[('Charles Joseph Minard, map of Napoleon\'s 1812 campaign', 'Trace the line in both directions and read the temperature below as a second narrative.', 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minard.png'), ('Florence Nightingale, mortality diagram', 'Notice that area, colour and month are doing different jobs.', 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nightingale-mortality.jpg'), ('W. E. B. Du Bois, <i>The Georgia Negro</i>, 1900', 'Look at the deliberate palette, labels and ornamental structure around quantitative comparisons.', 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Georgia_Negro_LCCN2013650428.jpg'), ('Edward Tufte, <i>The Visual Display of Quantitative Information</i>', 'Use his questions about integrity and comparison, not a ban on all visual pleasure.', 'https://www.edwardtufte.com/book/the-visual-display-of-quantitative-information/')],
         steal=['Give every chart a sentence that names the comparison it must make.', 'Use one visual channel for the main quantity and reserve colour or ornament for grouping, context or emphasis.'],
         director='Treat an on-screen map or graphic as a scene with a reading order. Reveal the variable that changes the character\'s understanding, not every number at once.',
         studio='A safari report can show sightings, habitat and season together, but label missing data and sampling limits. A beautiful map must not imply more certainty than the field notes support.',
         sources=[('Wikimedia Commons, Minard map record', 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minard.png'), ('Friendly and Andrews, Nightingale diagrams', 'https://doi.org/10.2436/20.8080.02.106'), ('Library of Congress, Du Bois charts', 'https://www.loc.gov/pictures/collection/anedub/'), ('Tufte, <i>The Visual Display of Quantitative Information</i>', 'https://www.edwardtufte.com/book/the-visual-display-of-quantitative-information/')]),

    dict(id='5.8', title='Pattern is a memory system',
         q='Can pattern and texture carry identity?',
         why='A repeated motif can make a surface recognisable before a name is read. But pattern is never just decoration: it carries material, place, labour, history and sometimes the prestige or politics of a brand. Identity comes from a grammar of repetition, variation and use.',
         plates=[dict(key='p58_morris', cap='Morris makes birds, fruit and foliage repeat without becoming a mechanical grid. Track the module and the small changes that keep it alive.'), dict(key='p58_tile', cap='Islamic geometric tilework turns a limited set of shapes into an apparently endless field. Look for the construction, not an exotic mood.')],
         story=[
             'William Morris\'s <i>Strawberry Thief</i> turns block printing, indigo discharge, birds and garden observation into a pattern with a strong visual signature. Islamic geometric tiling builds identity through proportion, symmetry, colour and repeat, often making local architecture feel continuous from wall to ceiling. Indian block printing and Sanganeri textiles similarly show how craft process, motif, pigment and region can become a recognisable family without every surface being identical.',
             'Modern brands borrow the same mechanism. Marimekko uses large, memorable motifs whose scale and colour can carry recognition. Louis Vuitton\'s monogram makes repetition itself a luxury signal, but it also shows the risk: once a pattern is widely copied, identity can become logo saturation. A pattern system needs rules for module, spacing, colour, scale and where to stop. Texture can support a brand, but it cannot repair a generic idea.',
             'Build a small tile, then vary only one thing at a time: rotation, scale, interval, colour or crop. Test it as a background, a border, a garment, a loading state and a billboard fragment. Preserve legibility and give the pattern a quiet field to breathe against. For MTRX, the strongest identity may be a restrained motif derived from habitat, craft or local material, credited honestly and used with permission, not a borrowed cultural surface pasted on as atmosphere. Ask who made the reference, what the motif means in its original setting and whether the maker should be credited or paid. Pattern becomes identity when repeated choices build recognition and relationship. Without that care, repetition can turn living craft into a costume that the audience reads as generic.',
         ],
         look=[('William Morris, <i>Strawberry Thief</i>', 'Study the repeat, the indigo process and the balance between animal, fruit and foliage.', 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morris_Strawberry_Thief_1883.jpg'), ('Jameh Mosque, Kerman, tile detail', 'See how geometry, colour and architecture make a field rather than an isolated ornament.', 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerm%C3%A1n,_mezquita_(2000)_09.jpg'), ('Marimekko, <i>Unikko</i>', 'A single flower becomes a flexible identity through scale and colour variation.', 'https://www.marimekko.com/us_en/design-stories/unikko'), ('Louis Vuitton, monogram history', 'Notice how repetition turns initials into surface, status and recognisable rhythm.', 'https://us.louisvuitton.com/eng-us/magazine/articles/the-monogram')],
         steal=['Define a repeat unit and three allowed transformations before commissioning a pattern library.', 'Use pattern at one memorable touchpoint and leave adjacent space quiet enough for recognition.'],
         director='Pattern can establish a world before a character speaks: wallpaper, cloth, tile or foliage creates affinity, then one broken repeat announces change.',
         studio='Derive a motif from a real habitat or craft reference, credit the source and explain the translation. Avoid treating Indian or Islamic pattern as a generic exotic texture.',
         sources=[('V&A, William Morris Strawberry Thief', 'https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O270884/strawberry-thief-furnishing-fabric-morris-william/'), ('V&A, Arts and Crafts', 'https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/arts-and-crafts-an-introduction'), ('Met, Islamic art and geometry overview', 'https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/geom/hd_geom.htm'), ('Marimekko, Unikko story', 'https://www.marimekko.com/us_en/design-stories/unikko')]),
]

# Each added paragraph keeps the lite stops concise while giving the visual idea enough room to travel from artwork to practice.
STOPS[0]['story'].append('A useful centre is often a negotiated centre. The subject may sit on the axis while the gaze, shadow or sound points elsewhere. That second direction prevents the frame from becoming a diagram. In a clinic page, a centred doctor can establish trust while an offset patient story supplies human context. In a film, a symmetrical room can hold an actor still until a small movement changes the meaning of every line around them. Design the axis, then design the pressure that acts on it.')
STOPS[1]['story'].append('Diagonals also organise time. A rising line can promise arrival, while a descending line can make the viewer anticipate impact. Repetition turns one diagonal into rhythm: poles, windows, limbs or cuts can echo it until a horizontal interruption feels like a decision. If the viewer cannot tell where the line begins or what it connects, the energy becomes visual noise. Give the diagonal an origin, a destination and a reason to be there.')
STOPS[2]['story'].append('Light can therefore be planned as a sequence, not a single look. Start with the room\'s normal value pattern, choose the first detail the audience must find and reserve the strongest contrast for the turn. A soft fill may keep a face humane; a hard edge may separate a silhouette from a background. The point is not to make every frame dramatic. It is to make the distribution of attention change when the story changes.')
STOPS[3]['story'].append('Recognition is also cultural and contextual. A mark that reads instantly to one audience may need a label, colour or surrounding word in another setting. Test the image with the actual names, languages, devices and distances of the project. For an animal identity, preserve the feature that distinguishes the species, not only the feature that looks elegant in a moodboard. Abstraction earns its calm by doing more work with fewer signals.')
STOPS[4]['story'].append('The camera also changes the viewer\'s responsibility. A wide frame can ask us to search and make a discovery feel earned; a close-up can remove escape routes and make a reaction unavoidable. Focus pulls can hand attention from one plane to another without cutting, while a locked frame can make movement inside it feel unusually important. Treat these choices as verbs in the scene: reveal, withhold, compress, release or confront.')
STOPS[5]['story'].append('A durable responsive system records its decisions. For each component, note the minimum readable size, the safe crop, the order of content and the alternative when space disappears. Test with real copy rather than placeholder rectangles, because language length is part of composition. A page that looks beautiful only with English headlines and perfect images is not a responsive composition. It is a single successful screenshot.')
STOPS[6]['story'].append('The chart maker is composing an argument, so the missing data matters as much as the visible mark. A gap may mean no observation, no report or no event, and those are different stories. State the unit, time window and denominator near the graphic. Then invite a second reading: can someone check the claim without trusting your colour choices? This is where visual beauty and scientific honesty reinforce each other rather than compete.')
STOPS[7]['story'].append('Pattern also has a scale problem. A repeat that feels intimate on cloth may become visual noise on a wall or disappear on a phone. Set a quiet scale, a strong scale and a cropping rule, then preview the pattern beside the type, photography and navigation it must coexist with. The identity is not the tile alone. It is the relationship between the repeated surface and the moments when the surface stops so the message can speak.')
STOPS[1]['story'][-1] += ' In a poster, that reason can be a single word or face; in a landscape, it may be the path the eye takes before it finds the subject.'
STOPS[3]['story'][-1] += ' The goal is not fewer marks at any cost. It is a stable relationship between cue, category and context.'
STOPS[4]['story'][-1] += ' A shot list that records these verbs gives the crew a shared reason for a technical choice.'
STOPS[5]['story'][-1] += ' Keep the content model flexible enough that the visual hierarchy can change without rewriting the whole page.'
STOPS[7]['story'][-1] += ' Let the surface support recognition, not compete with the words that explain what the visitor should do.'
STOPS[4]['story'][-1] += ' The frame is a designed boundary, not a neutral window.'
STOPS[5]['story'][-1] += ' Build those rules into the component documentation and review them whenever the content changes.'
STOPS[6]['story'][-1] += ' A clear caption can save more confusion than another decorative layer.'
STOPS[4]['story'][-1] += ' It decides what the audience can know and when, which is why camera craft is also story craft.'
